<p>Sorry. You don't own &quot;old timey Englishman explorer has fantastical adventures.&quot; And I'll be honest, that wasn't an original idea on the part of Lord Blackwood, either. What made Lord Blackwood stand out was the fact that he's a sea slug that's also an old timey Englishman explorer. You added a twist to a trope that was established back in the 19th century. You didn't invent it. As such, your assertion that this article <em>is</em>, not just <em>is like</em> Lord Blackwood is pretty baseless.</p>
<p>I'll go further and say that it's unfair to the author that you're coming into the discussion thread and accusing him of ripping you off (and adding in the same characterization of <a href="/scp-1958">SCP-1958</a> for good measure), when there is no evidence of that being true at all. Plagiarism, whether literal or conceptual, is a pretty serious charge to throw around, and if you're going to accuse someone of it, I think it requires a higher standard of evidence than what you're offering.</p>

